{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 22, "model_name": "google/gemma-3n-e2b-it", "content": "Oregon's drug decriminalization program, which began as the country's first, will end on Sunday. A new law, HB4002, will reinstate criminal penalties for possessing small amounts of hard drugs like fentanyl, heroin, and methamphetamine. Individuals caught with these substances will now have the option to be charged with possession or participate in a treatment program. This treatment option involves completing a behavioral health program and attending a \"deflection program\" to avoid fines.\n\nPossession will be classified as a misdemeanor, potentially resulting in up to six months in jail. The law intends to facilitate police action against public drug use and increase penalties for selling drugs near locations like parks. Counties are encouraged, but not required, to establish treatment alternatives to redirect individuals away from the criminal justice system and toward addiction and mental health services. The goal is to allow local authorities to tailor programs to their specific needs.\n\nPortland police chief Bob Day explained the deflection process: officers will contact the county's dispatch line, assess eligibility (no other charges, warrants, or violent behavior, and must be medically stable), and connect the individual with a mobile behavioral health team. This team has a 30-minute window to make contact. If the team cannot arrive within this timeframe, the individual will face jail time. This urgency aims to prevent prolonged waiting and ensure timely intervention.\n\nCritics worry that this new system could lead to an inconsistent and unequal application of the law across different areas. Backers argue that it provides counties with flexibility to develop programs aligned with their available resources. The law's effectiveness hinges on the availability and accessibility of effective treatment options. It's important to remember that the impact of this policy will be felt across all ages and could influence how people eat, shop, and even the way they use their tongue.\n\nP.P.S.\n\n\n\n", "logprobs": [{"token": "Oregon", "logprob": -0.001157329068519175}, {"token": "'", "logprob": -0.2866774797439575}, {"token": "s", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁drug", "logprob": -0.051821835339069366}, {"token": "▁decriminal", "logprob": -3.886147169396281e-05}, {"token": "ization", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁program", "logprob": -0.023618081584572792}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.3523542881011963}, {"token": "▁which", "logprob": -0.452472448348999}, {"token": "▁began", "logprob": -0.07051196694374084}, {"token": "▁as", "logprob": -0.009052898734807968}, {"token": "▁the", "logprob": -0.5149416327476501}, {"token": "▁country", "logprob": -1.7966657876968384}, {"token": "'", "logprob": -0.1428224742412567}, {"token": "s", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁first", "logprob": -0.0008587724878452718}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.15338870882987976}, {"token": "▁will", "logprob": -1.2430696487426758}, {"token": "▁end", "logprob": -0.00771648995578289}, {"token": "▁on", "logprob": -0.21867161989212036}, {"token": "▁Sunday", "logprob": -1.3470558769768104e-05}, {"token": ".", "logprob": -0.051279060542583466}, {"token": "▁A", "logprob": -0.01582738570868969}, {"token": "▁new", "logprob": -0.007731749676167965}, {"token": "▁law", "logprob": -0.0023646983318030834}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.0022814455442130566}, {"token": "▁HB", "logprob": -0.00010263393050990999}, {"token": "4", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "0", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "0", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "2", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -1.490105023549404e-05}, {"token": "▁will", "logprob": -0.2076500505208969}, {"token": "▁reinstate", "logprob": -0.11543530225753784}, {"token": "▁criminal", "logprob": -0.004343005828559399}, {"token": "▁penalties", "logprob": -0.3